    <section class="cd-sec">
        <span class="visa-pill">Know your plan</span>
        <h2>Does Your Plan Cover China?</h2>
        <p>Many international plans cover treatment in China — including <strong>Bupa, MSH, and Allianz</strong>. But coverage is not automatic. The two things that determine whether your claim is paid are:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Direct billing vs reimbursement:</strong> whether the hospital can bill your insurer directly, or you pay first and claim back.</li>
            <li><strong>Pre-authorisation:</strong> whether you must get approval before treatment.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Check your policy's <strong>China network</strong> and its pre-authorisation rules before booking anything.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="cd-sec">
        <span class="visa-pill">Two paths</span>
        <h2>Direct Billing vs Reimbursement</h2>
        <table class="visa-table">
            <tr><th></th><th>Direct billing</th><th>Reimbursement (pay &amp; claim)</th></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>How it works</strong></td><td>Hospital bills insurer directly</td><td>You pay, then submit a claim</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Up-front cost</strong></td><td>Little or none</td><td>You pay full amount first</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Where it applies</strong></td><td>Hospitals in your insurer's network</td><td>Most Chinese public hospitals</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Paperwork</strong></td><td>Minimal</td><td>Itemized invoices + reports</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p style="margin-top:10px;">Most public hospitals in China are <strong>reimbursement-only</strong> for international insurers — you pay at the cashier and claim back. Some international departments and private hospitals accept direct billing from major insurers. We confirm which applies to your matched hospital.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="cd-sec">
        <h2>Pre-Authorisation: Get Approval First</h2>
        <p>For planned treatment like surgery, most insurers require <strong>pre-authorisation</strong>. Skipping it is the single most common reason claims are rejected.</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Obtain a written diagnosis and a treatment plan from your home doctor (or a Chinese hospital).</li>
            <li>Get a cost estimate for the planned treatment.</li>
            <li>Submit both to your insurer and wait for written approval.</li>
            <li>Keep the approval letter — you may need to show it at the hospital.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>We provide the treatment plan and cost estimate documents your insurer requests.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="cd-sec">
        <h2>Documents You Need for Reimbursement</h2>
        <p>When you pay first and claim back, your claim needs to be complete. Collect all of these before you leave the hospital:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Itemized hospital invoices</strong> (every receipt, not just the final total).</li>
            <li><strong>Discharge summary</strong> — what was done, the diagnosis, and dates.</li>
            <li><strong>Diagnostic reports</strong> — imaging (MRI/CT) and lab results.</li>
            <li><strong>Treating doctor's letter</strong> — sometimes required to explain medical necessity.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:10px;">Chinese hospitals issue these in Chinese; some insurers require <strong>certified translations</strong>. We help collect and organise the full set so your claim is not delayed or rejected for missing paperwork.</p>
    </section>

    <h2 class="sp-section-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="sp-faq">
        <div class="sp-faq-item"><h3>Does my international health insurance cover treatment in China?</h3><p>Many international plans (Bupa, MSH, Allianz) cover China, but it depends on your policy's network and whether pre-authorisation is required. Check your China network before booking.</p></div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item"><h3>What is the difference between direct billing and reimbursement?</h3><p>Direct billing means the hospital bills your insurer (little up-front cost); reimbursement means you pay first and claim back. Most Chinese public hospitals are reimbursement-only for international insurers.</p></div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item"><h3>Do I need pre-authorisation before treatment in China?</h3><p>For planned treatment like surgery, most insurers require it. Submit your diagnosis, treatment plan, and cost estimate for approval before treatment — skipping it is the top cause of rejected claims.</p></div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item"><h3>What documents do I need to claim reimbursement?</h3><p>Itemized invoices, discharge summary, diagnostic reports, and sometimes a treating doctor's letter — often with certified translations. We help collect and organise the complete set.</p></div>
    </div>

